Interface#h1

Ecmascript module loader for commands and procedures. 

2024


{text-align:justify;margin:0 10%;}
  Websites have become humanly unmaintainable. 

  No wonder artificial boilerplate is sweeping the industry. 

  The javascript platform has been increasingly convoluted by non-standard syntactic variations in its ecosystem (commonjs, flow, typescript, purescript, jsx, templates &c.), requiring growing amounts of configuration to know and maintain (package manifest, transpiler, bundler, linter &c.). Even when these have merit (static analysis, optimization), they are at the expense of javascript's flexibility, which could provide these advantages natively. To make matters worse, package managers (npm, yarn, deno) have long obscured configurations of external modules, contributing to overengineering on the web, rendering unreadable a language that could be distributed in source format (so to servers as to browsers). 

  Does javascript really have to be this complicated? 


{text-align:center;}
(Simplicity is the courage to express what we think.)#b 
{text-align:justify;line-height:1.2em}


 With the arrival of loader commands in the (Ecmascript specification)@https://whatwg.github.io/loader/{"style":{"&+.media":{"filter":"invert(0.8)"}}} and their implementation in Node.js ((version 16)@https://nodejs.org/api/module.html#customization-hooks), volatile dialects can finally be standardized dynamically, without a need for external configurations or a new platform trying to contain this heterogeneity ((UMD)@https://jameshfisher.com/2020/10/04/what-are-umd-modules/, Deno@https://www.youtube.com/embed/M3BM9TB-8yA, Bun@https://bun.sh/docs). 

 Modules consist of imported#b, exported#b and local#b declarations. Procedural statements are allowed due to javascript's lack of an interface to access declarations. Interface#b corrects this with a single procedure that accesses declarations and infers#i arguments when provided. Take a simple module with a function to cumulate numbers: 

