(Module loading)#b takes 3 steps: 

 (1. locate:)#b test availability of module, if not found use source definition to redirect, download, access and delete source. Imported by Node as "resolve"#b. 

 (2. access:)#b syntactic evaluation. Includes denotation if foreign dialect detected or specified by source definition. Imported by Node as "load"#b. 

 (3. interpret:)#b semantic evaluation. Not imported by nodejs; used in the load step to run tests, or directly for (manual loading)@https://github.com/jsinterface/interface/issues/26. 

 By default, Node rejects module specifiers that aren't found, necessitating separate package management. Interface thus expands location#i to remote entry points (file, archive or sparse git branch) if declared for the given relative specifier in a (source definition)#b (sources.json), along with its parsing/bundling specification (dialect, alias, edit, replace, patch): 

