 After resolving#b the input modules and agent#b specifier, the expose function scans these module namespaces for required variables. The sensitive protocol and certification files are classified#b to be denied access on vulnerable routes. Routes can be of two types: the "default" passive http routes#b, and active websocket "actions"#b to broadcast. In addition, they may expose empty "syndication"#b (API) and "encryption"#b keys it requires from the protocol module. If a certification#b is not found for the https protocol, the distinguishedname#b is used to send a certificate signing request to Letsencrypt over ACME protocol, or generate a self-signed one. Lastly a function composition binds the specified port#b to a router#b function receiving incoming requests and routing them to values on the specified cache#b, routes#b or websocket actions#b. The values are then staged#b with appropriate headers and submitted#b as response. If more certificates are provided, they are assigned to the host context#b to recognize each origin (domain name)#b in requests (with a little change, they could also be served different routes). 

